<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Project Management</span>
      <h1>Linear vs Jira: Which Project Tool Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Linear and Jira are the two project management tools most often shortlisted by software development teams in 2026, and they take opposite approaches. Linear wins on speed, keyboard-first workflows, and a clean modern interface. Jira wins on customization, enterprise compliance, and integration with the broader Atlassian suite. This comparison breaks down issues, sprints, roadmaps, pricing, and integrations so you can pick the right one for your team.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://linear.app" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Linear Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Jira Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Linear</th><th>Jira</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Modern software teams, startups</td><td>Enterprises, complex workflows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Up to 250 issues</td><td>Up to 10 users</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Standard Plan</td><td>$8/user/mo</td><td>$7.16/user/mo (under 10 users)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Fastest in category</td><td>Slower, especially at scale</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Keyboard Shortcuts</td><td>Best in class, command palette</td><td>Limited, mouse-heavy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Issues and Tasks</td><td>Issues, sub-issues, projects</td><td>Issues, sub-tasks, epics, stories</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sprints</td><td>Cycles</td><td>Sprints (Scrum boards)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Roadmap</td><td>Built-in, visual</td><td>Advanced Roadmaps (Premium)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Custom Workflows</td><td>Limited, opinionated</td><td>Highly customizable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Integrations</td><td>GitHub, Slack, Figma, Notion</td><td>200+ apps, Atlassian suite</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API</td><td>GraphQL, modern</td><td>REST, mature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile Apps</td><td>iOS, Android</td><td>iOS, Android</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compliance</td><td>SOC 2, GDPR</td><td>SOC 2, GDPR, FedRAMP</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Linear?</h2>
    <p>Linear is a project management tool built specifically for software development teams, founded in 2019 by Karri Saarinen, Tuomas Artman, and Jori Lallo. The team previously worked at Uber, Airbnb, and Microsoft, and they built Linear to fix what they saw as broken in Jira: slow performance, cluttered interface, and workflows designed for managers rather than developers. Linear is now used by over 10,000 companies including Vercel, Cash App, and Ramp, and it has become the default choice for modern startups that prioritize developer experience.</p>
    <p>The current version supports issues, sub-issues, projects, cycles (sprints), roadmaps, and integrations with GitHub, Slack, Figma, and Notion. Linear's defining feature is speed: the app responds in under 100ms, supports a command palette for keyboard-first navigation, and syncs changes instantly across all users via a real-time sync engine. Linear is opinionated, with a fixed workflow that prioritizes simplicity over customization, which means less configuration overhead but less flexibility than Jira. Pricing starts at free for up to 250 issues, Standard at $8 per user per month, Plus at $14, and Enterprise for large organizations.</p>

    <h2>What Is Jira?</h2>
    <p>Jira is a project management tool built by Atlassian, launched in 2002 by Mike Cannon-Brookes and Scott Farquhar. It started as a bug tracker and grew into the dominant project management tool for software development, with over 100,000 customers as of 2026. Jira is bundled with the Atlassian suite, which includes Confluence, Bitbucket, Trello, and Compass, and it targets enterprises and mid-sized organizations that need customization and compliance.</p>
    <p>The current version supports issues, sub-tasks, epics, stories, sprints, Kanban boards, Scrum boards, roadmaps, and Advanced Roadmaps on Premium plans. Jira is highly customizable: you can create custom workflows with 20+ statuses, custom fields for every team, and automation rules that integrate with the entire Atlassian suite. Jira is slower than Linear, especially at scale, but it handles complexity that Linear cannot. Pricing starts at free for up to 10 users, Standard at $7.16 per user per month for small teams, Premium at $13.53, and Enterprise for large organizations.</p>

    <h2>Speed and Interface</h2>
    <p>How fast the tool responds defines the daily experience. Linear is the fastest project tool in 2026, while Jira is slower especially at scale.</p>

    <h3>Linear Speed</h3>
    <p>Linear's app responds in under 100ms for every action. Creating an issue, assigning it, moving it between sprints, and updating status all happen instantly. The command palette (Cmd+K) lets you navigate, create, and update issues without touching the mouse. For developers who value speed and keyboard-first workflows, Linear is best in class.</p>

    <h3>Jira Interface</h3>
    <p>Jira's interface is functional but slower. Creating an issue opens a modal with multiple fields, and saving takes a noticeable second. The board view is heavier than Linear, and search can be slow on large instances. Jira compensates with customization: you can configure every field, workflow, and view to match your team's exact process.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span> wins speed and interface.</p>

    <h2>Issues and Workflow</h2>
    <p>How issues are structured and how workflows operate defines the tool's flexibility. Linear is opinionated, while Jira is infinitely customizable.</p>

    <h3>Linear Issues</h3>
    <p>Linear issues support sub-issues, projects, and cycles. The workflow is fixed: Backlog, Todo, In Progress, In Review, Done, Canceled. You can add custom labels and priorities, but the core workflow is opinionated. This is a feature for teams that want simplicity, but a limitation for teams that need custom workflows.</p>

    <h3>Jira Issues</h3>
    <p>Jira issues support sub-tasks, epics, stories, bugs, and custom issue types. Workflows are fully customizable: you can create workflows with 20+ statuses, transitions with conditions and validators, and custom fields for every team. Jira also supports custom screens, so different issue types can have different fields. For teams with complex process requirements, Jira is the only choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best simplicity goes to <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span>. Best customization goes to <span class="winner-tag">Jira</span>.</p>

    <h2>Sprints and Agile Workflows</h2>
    <p>Both tools support agile sprints, but Linear's Cycles are simpler while Jira's Scrum boards are more configurable.</p>

    <h3>Linear Cycles</h3>
    <p>Linear's Cycles are time-boxed periods, typically 2 weeks, where teams commit to a set of issues. Linear tracks progress, velocity, and burndown automatically. You can drag issues between cycles, and Linear remembers historical velocity for forecasting. The cycle view is clean and focuses on what matters: what is in this sprint, what is at risk, and what is done.</p>

    <h3>Jira Sprints</h3>
    <p>Jira's Sprints are part of Scrum boards, which support sprints, backlogs, and velocity charts. Jira's sprint planning is more configurable: you can set sprint goals, capacity, and start/end dates. The velocity chart is more detailed than Linear's, and Jira supports sprint retrospective reports. For teams that follow strict Scrum, Jira's sprint tooling is more mature.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best simplicity goes to <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span>. Best Scrum maturity goes to <span class="winner-tag">Jira</span>.</p>

    <h2>Roadmaps and Planning</h2>
    <p>Roadmaps show the long-term plan. Linear's roadmap is built-in, while Jira's Advanced Roadmaps require Premium.</p>

    <h3>Linear Roadmap</h3>
    <p>Linear's roadmap view shows projects and issues across quarters. You can group by project, status, or team, and the roadmap updates automatically as issues progress. The roadmap is included in all paid plans, which makes it accessible to smaller teams.</p>

    <h3>Jira Advanced Roadmaps</h3>
    <p>Jira's Advanced Roadmaps, available on Premium and Enterprise, supports cross-project planning, capacity management, and what-if scenarios. It is more powerful than Linear's roadmap but also more complex. For organizations that plan across multiple teams and projects, Advanced Roadmaps is the better tool.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best simple roadmap goes to <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span>. Best advanced planning goes to <span class="winner-tag">Jira</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is comparable, but Linear includes more features in its base plan while Jira charges extra for Premium.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Linear</th><th>Jira</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>Up to 250 issues</td><td>Up to 10 users</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Standard</td><td>$8/user/mo</td><td>$7.16/user/mo (under 10 users)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Premium</td><td>$14/user/mo (Plus)</td><td>$13.53/user/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>Custom</td><td>Custom</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Roadmap</td><td>All paid plans</td><td>Premium and above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Advanced Automations</td><td>All paid plans</td><td>Premium and above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Code Insights</td><td>Plus and above</td><td>Premium and above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sandbox</td><td>Not available</td><td>Premium and above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>24/7 Support</td><td>Enterprise</td><td>Premium and above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annual Billing Discount</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, varies</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For small teams under 10 users, both tools offer a free tier. Linear's free tier is limited by issues (250), while Jira's is limited by users (10). For teams that need paid features, Linear Standard at $8 per user per month is slightly more expensive than Jira Standard at $7.16, but Linear includes roadmap and automations in Standard, while Jira requires Premium at $13.53 for Advanced Roadmaps.</p>
    <p>For larger organizations, Jira's per-user pricing is competitive, but the cost of Premium features adds up. A 100-person team on Linear Plus pays $1,400 per month, while the same team on Jira Premium pays $1,353 per month.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best free tier for small teams goes to <span class="winner-tag">Jira</span>. Best value for features goes to <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span>.</p>

    <h2>Integrations and Ecosystem</h2>
    <p>Both tools integrate with developer tools, but Jira has a larger ecosystem through the Atlassian Marketplace.</p>

    <h3>Linear Integrations</h3>
    <p>Linear integrates with GitHub, GitLab, Slack, Figma, Notion, Front, Discord, and Zendesk. The GitHub integration is particularly strong: when you mention a Linear issue in a PR title or branch name, Linear automatically links the PR and updates status when it merges. Linear's API is GraphQL, which is modern and well-documented.</p>

    <h3>Jira Integrations</h3>
    <p>Jira integrates with 200+ apps through the Atlassian Marketplace, including GitHub, GitLab, Slack, Microsoft Teams, Figma, Confluence, Bitbucket, and Compass. Jira's deep integration with the Atlassian suite is a major advantage for organizations that use Confluence for documentation and Bitbucket for code. Jira's API is REST, which is mature but less modern than Linear's GraphQL.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best modern integrations go to <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span>. Best ecosystem size goes to <span class="winner-tag">Jira</span>.</p>

    <h2>Mobile and Offline Experience</h2>
    <p>Developers do not just work at their desks, and project tools need to function on phones and tablets too. Linear and Jira both have mobile apps, but the experience is very different.</p>

    <h3>Linear Mobile</h3>
    <p>Linear's iOS and Android apps mirror the speed of the desktop app. Creating an issue, assigning it, and updating status all happen in under 100ms, and the command palette is available on mobile via a long-press gesture. Linear's mobile app also supports push notifications for mentions and assignments, and the search is as fast as desktop. The app's dark mode is well-designed and matches the desktop theme automatically. Offline support is limited: you can read cached issues, but creating or editing issues requires a connection. For developers who triage issues on the go, Linear mobile is the best in the category.</p>

    <h3>Jira Mobile</h3>
    <p>Jira's mobile app is functional but slower than Linear. Creating an issue on mobile requires filling out multiple fields, and the modal can feel heavy on a small screen. Jira compensates with stronger offline support: you can create and edit issues offline, and changes sync when you reconnect. Jira mobile also supports board views, sprint management, and comment threads. For project managers who need to review boards and approve changes from a phone, Jira mobile works. For developers who want fast issue triage, Linear is clearly better and faster.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best mobile speed goes to <span class="winner-tag">Linear</span>. Best offline and board access go to <span class="winner-tag">Jira</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Linear Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Fastest project tool in 2026</li>
        <li>Keyboard-first with command palette</li>
        <li>Clean, modern interface that developers love</li>
        <li>Built-in roadmap on all paid plans</li>
        <li>Strong GitHub integration with automatic PR linking</li>
        <li>Modern GraphQL API</li>
        <li>Native Jira migration tool</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Linear Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Free tier limited to 250 issues</li>
        <li>Limited customization for workflows</li>
        <li>No sandbox environment</li>
        <li>Fewer integrations than Jira</li>
        <li>No service desk or ITSM features</li>
        <li>Less mature for enterprises over 500 people</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Jira Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Highly customizable workflows and fields</li>
        <li>Best ecosystem with 200+ marketplace apps</li>
        <li>Deep integration with Atlassian suite</li>
        <li>Advanced Roadmaps for cross-project planning</li>
        <li>Sandbox environment on Premium</li>
        <li>FedRAMP compliance for government</li>
        <li>Jira Service Management for ITSM</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Jira Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Slower performance, especially at scale</li>
        <li>Interface feels heavy and cluttered</li>
        <li>Mouse-heavy, limited keyboard support</li>
        <li>Advanced features require Premium plan</li>
        <li>Steeper learning curve for new teams</li>
        <li>Configuration can take weeks for complex setups</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Startups and Modern Software Teams</h3>
      <p>If you are a startup or modern software team under 200 people that values speed and developer experience, Linear is the better choice. The keyboard-first workflow, fast performance, and clean interface make developers want to use the tool instead of avoiding it. Startups and product-led teams consistently prefer Linear.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Enterprises with Complex Workflows</h3>
      <p>If you are an enterprise over 500 people with complex workflows, custom processes, and compliance requirements, Jira is the better choice. The customization options, Atlassian suite integration, and enterprise features like sandbox and FedRAMP compliance handle complexity that Linear cannot. Enterprises consistently prefer Jira.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Teams That Use Confluence and Bitbucket</h3>
      <p>If your team already uses Confluence for documentation and Bitbucket for code, Jira is the natural choice. The deep integration between the three tools creates a unified workflow: Jira issues link to Confluence pages, Bitbucket PRs update Jira status automatically, and cross-tool search works seamlessly. Switching to Linear means breaking this integration.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Teams That Value Developer Experience</h3>
      <p>If your team values developer experience and wants a tool that developers actually enjoy using, Linear is the better choice. The speed, keyboard shortcuts, and clean interface make Linear feel like a native part of the developer workflow rather than overhead. Developer-led teams consistently prefer Linear.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Teams That Need ITSM or Service Desk</h3>
      <p>If you need IT service management or a service desk for ticketing, Jira is the better choice. Jira Service Management integrates with Jira Software, so you can handle both development work and IT tickets in one platform. Linear does not have a service desk product, so you would need a separate tool like Zendesk or Front.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>20-person startup shipping weekly releases</h3>
      <p>A 20-person startup ships weekly releases and needs fast, lightweight project tracking. Linear's keyboard-first workflow lets engineers create, assign, and update issues in seconds without leaving the keyboard. Cycles track weekly progress, and the roadmap shows the quarterly plan to investors. The team moves fast without tool overhead. Jira would work but feels heavier and slower for a team this size.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>1,000-person enterprise with regulated workflows</h3>
      <p>A 1,000-person enterprise needs project tracking with custom workflows for compliance, integration with Confluence documentation, and a sandbox for testing changes. Jira's custom workflows handle the compliance requirements, Confluence integration links issues to documentation, and the sandbox lets IT test changes before production. Linear cannot match this level of customization.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Product team migrating from Jira to Linear</h3>
      <p>A 50-person product team migrates from Jira to Linear to improve developer experience. Linear's native migration tool imports 3,000 issues with comments, attachments, and history in 2 hours. The team adapts to Linear's keyboard-first workflow in a week and reports 30% faster issue creation. The trade-off is losing some Jira customizations, which the team accepts in exchange for speed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>DevOps team using Atlassian suite</h3>
      <p>A DevOps team uses Jira Software for development, Jira Service Management for incident response, Confluence for runbooks, and Bitbucket for code. The integrated workflow links incidents to Jira issues, runbooks to deployment pipelines, and PRs to sprint tasks. Switching to Linear would break this integration, so the team stays on Jira despite preferring Linear's interface.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for modern software teams: Linear.</strong> Linear Standard at $8 per user per month offers the fastest, cleanest, most developer-friendly project tool in 2026. For startups, product-led teams, and any software team under 500 people that values speed and developer experience, Linear is the safer and higher-quality choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for enterprises and complex workflows: Jira.</strong> Jira Standard at $7.16 per user per month offers the customization, ecosystem, and enterprise features that large organizations need. For enterprises over 500 people, regulated industries, and teams already using the Atlassian suite, Jira is the better choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Best budget choice for small teams: Jira.</strong> Jira's free tier supports up to 10 users with unlimited issues, which is more generous than Linear's 250-issue limit. For small teams under 10 users that want to start free, Jira is the clear winner.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For organizations that need both speed and enterprise features, run Linear for product and engineering teams and Jira Service Management for IT and incident response. Total cost is around $15 per user per month for users who need both, but most users only need one or the other.</p>
    </div>

