    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Linear better than Jira?</h3>
    <p>Linear is better for modern software teams that want speed, keyboard-first workflows, and a clean interface. Jira is better for larger enterprises that need customization, compliance, and integration with the broader Atlassian suite. For startups and product-led teams under 200 people, Linear wins. For enterprises over 500 people with complex workflows, Jira wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Linear or Jira?</h3>
    <p>Pricing is comparable. Linear Standard costs $8 per user per month, while Jira Standard costs $7.16 per user per month for teams under 10 users and $7.75 per user per month for teams over 10. Linear includes more features in its base plan, while Jira charges extra for Premium features like advanced roadmaps and automation.</p>

    <h3>Can Linear replace Jira?</h3>
    <p>Yes, for most modern software teams under 500 people. Linear handles issues, sprints, roadmaps, projects, and cycles, which covers the core Jira workflow. Teams that need Jira-specific features like custom workflows with 20+ statuses, advanced service desk, or deep integration with Confluence and Bitbucket may need to stay on Jira.</p>

    <h3>Does Linear support agile sprints?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Linear supports agile sprints through its Cycles feature. Cycles are time-boxed periods (typically 2 weeks) where teams commit to a set of issues. Linear tracks progress, velocity, and burndown automatically. Linear also supports projects for longer-running work that spans multiple cycles.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from Jira to Linear?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Linear has a native Jira migration tool that imports issues, comments, attachments, and history. The migration preserves issue keys, status, assignees, and labels. Most teams complete migration in a few hours for projects under 5,000 issues. Larger migrations may require batching and cleanup before import.</p>

    <h3>Which has better GitHub integration?</h3>
    <p>Both integrate well with GitHub, but Linear's integration is more automated. When you mention a Linear issue in a GitHub PR title or branch name, Linear automatically links the PR, updates status when the PR merges, and closes the issue if configured. Jira requires more manual linking but supports deeper integration with Bitbucket and GitLab for enterprise workflows.</p>

